lessons.explanation:
## The server's one-number verdict

Every response starts with a three-digit status code. The FIRST digit is the family — learn the families and you can read any code you have never seen:

- **2xx — success.** `200 OK`, `201 Created` (something new exists, typically after POST), `204 No Content` (success, nothing to say — typical for DELETE).
- **3xx — redirection.** `301 Moved Permanently`, `302 Found` (temporary), `304 Not Modified` (your cache is still good).
- **4xx — the CLIENT messed up.** `400 Bad Request` (malformed input), `401 Unauthorized` (really means *unauthenticated* — who are you?), `403 Forbidden` (I know who you are — you may not), `404 Not Found`, `409 Conflict` (duplicate email, version clash), `429 Too Many Requests`.
- **5xx — the SERVER messed up.** `500 Internal Server Error` (unhandled exception), `502 Bad Gateway` / `503 Service Unavailable` (something behind the load balancer is down).

### Worked example: picking a code

```js
// POST /users { email: 'taken@x.io' }
// → body invalid?            400
// → no/bad credentials?      401
// → valid user, no rights?   403
// → email already exists?    409
// → insert threw?            500
// → all good, row inserted?  201 (+ the new user in the body)
```

Read it top to bottom: that IS the order a well-built handler checks things — validate, authenticate, authorize, check conflicts, do the work. Each failure mode has its own code so clients can react differently (retry? re-login? show a form error?).

### Common mistakes

- Returning 200 with `{ "error": "..." }` in the body. Clients, caches, and monitoring all key off the status code — make it tell the truth.
- Mixing up 401 (not logged in → authenticate) and 403 (logged in, not allowed → no point retrying).

